[FINAL-TEXT] **Major finding confirmed!**

**ispa `dhcpd.conf`**: `default-lease-time 600;` — **10-minute lease** on the WAN-facing interface!
**ispb `dhcpd.conf`**: `default-lease-time 600;` — same.

**r1 lease audit** (from `dhclient.leases`):
- eth2 lease: `option dhcp-lease-time 600` → renew at ~T/2 (5 min), expires at 10 min. Multiple completed renewals in history, confirming repeating renewal cycle.
- eth3 lease: same 600-second pattern.
- r1's default route `default via 203.0.113.1 dev eth2` is **DHCP-managed** (pushed by `option routers` in the lease).

**Mechanism:** Every ~5 minutes, dhclient on r1 must renew the lease on eth2. When dhclient processes the DHCPACK, it re-runs its interface scripts which momentarily remove and re-add the default route (`203.0.113.1 via eth2`). During this brief reconfiguration window, in-flight TCP connections from PC1 (which has only one default route through r1→ispa) stall until the route is restored. This produces exactly the "hangs for a bit and comes back" pattern the user reports.

This is a **DHCP server misconfiguration on ispa** (and identically ispb). Now writing Phase 5.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: ispa, ispb
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The DHCP server daemon (`dhcpd`) on both ISP devices (`ispa` and `ispb`) is misconfigured with an abnormally short `default-lease-time 600` (10 minutes) in `/etc/dhcp/dhcpd.conf`. These devices provide DHCP leases to router `r1`'s WAN-facing interfaces (`eth2` → ispa, `eth3` → ispb), pushing both the public IP address and the default-gateway option (`option routers`).

**Mechanism:** Because the lease duration is only 600 seconds, `r1`'s `dhclient` process must attempt a unicast RENEW at approximately T/2 (every ~5 minutes). When `dhclient` processes the DHCPACK and re-applies the lease, its interface scripts briefly remove and re-add the DHCP-managed default route (`default via 203.0.113.1 dev eth2`). During this brief reconfiguration window, no default route exists on `r1`, so internet-bound packets from PC1 are dropped. Active TCP connections stall; pages hang until the route is restored seconds later — matching the user's description of intermittent hangs that "come back on their own."

**Localization:** `ispa` is the primary faulty device: it is the DHCP server for `r1.eth2` (203.0.113.2/30) and its pushed gateway (`203.0.113.1`) is `r1`'s sole default route. `ispb` has the identical 600-second misconfiguration for `r1.eth3` (203.0.113.6/30), making both ISP DHCP servers the root of the fault.

**Root cause:** Both `ispa` and `ispb` are configured with `default-lease-time 600`, causing r1's WAN DHCP leases — and with them its internet default route — to be torn down and reconstructed every ~5 minutes.

**Fix:** On both `ispa` and `ispb`, increase `default-lease-time` in `/etc/dhcp/dhcpd.conf` to at least 3600 (1 hour), ideally 86400 (24 hours), then restart `dhcpd`. This will eliminate the frequent renewal cycle and stabilise `r1`'s default route.
=== END ===